  <div class="thesis-summary"><p><strong>AI needs dependable electricity.</strong> Data centres used an estimated 485 TWh globally in 2025. The IEA projects around 950 TWh by 2030.</p><p><strong>Energy security changes the equation.</strong> Oil supply disruptions strengthen the case for diversification and EVs, alongside investment in generation, grids and storage.</p></div>
  <details class="thesis-details"><summary>Read the thesis and sources</summary><div class="thesis-transcript">
    <h3>See the shift early.</h3><p>Who knows more, wins the next move. The next economy runs on electricity. Understanding demand can help us prepare the infrastructure it needs.</p>
    <h3>Electricity supports productive economies.</h3><p>Compare electricity consumption and GDP per person across seven selected economies using 2023 data.</p>
    <h3>Digital growth has a physical power demand.</h3><p>The IEA’s 2026 outlook estimates global data-centre electricity use at 485 TWh in 2025 and projects around 950 TWh in 2030. Its 2025 Base Case projects Southeast Asian data-centre electricity demand at more than twice the 2024 level by 2030.</p>
    <h3>Energy security has a supply-route risk.</h3><p>Before the crisis, around 60% of Southeast Asia’s crude oil imports came from the Middle East. Supply disruptions and price volatility reinforce the case for diversification.</p>
    <h3>Electrification changes what must be built.</h3><p>EVs can reduce exposure to oil, while adding demand for electricity and charging. Generation, grids, storage and reliable operations support that shift.</p>
